hook     [hunt1 sharp] A popular naval war game just admitted to copying an artist's vehicle design. And they are selling it to players for up to seven hundred dollars.
         stamp:$700 SHIP
who      [photo1 sharp] The game is Modern Warships. The creator is concept artist Alex Jay Brady, who drew the original flying ship back in twenty-fourteen.
         tag:Alex Jay Brady | stamp:2014 DESIGN
discovery [auto under] Brady spotted the ship in-game and posted the proof on X. The developers copied everything, including non-functional design mistakes like planes taking off backward.
         receipt:post1
cost     [hunt2 under] IGN reports the vehicle is locked behind gacha spins costing up to one hundred dollars, or seven hundred dollars to buy outright.
         blocks:2
artistreaction [auto under] Brady says they did not give permission, but wrote that if the studio had just sent some money, they would have zero issues.
         quote:Honestly if they'd just asked and sent me some $$$$ Id have had zero issue with them using it.
studioconfession [auto under] Then the developers did something rare. They publicly replied, apologized, and admitted they used the design as a reference without proper clearance.
         receipt:post0
whatsnext [hunt1 under] Kotaku reports the studio is not removing the vehicle. Instead, they are trying to reach a deal to pay the artist directly.
         rows:2
vote     [photo1 sharp] So what should happen? Should the studio pay the artist to keep it, or remove the design completely? Comment one word. Pay, or remove.
         chip:CHOOSE ONE
site     [auto under] The full timeline, and every receipt, is on Gen Z Hype. Link in bio.
         
